# Killing the pole at q=0 by a polynomial in j(q)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.exists_aeval_jq_sub_holomorphicAtInfty`
(`d6346d7d-ef2f-5365-bb82-a00369824747`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `67fb34af-b139-50be-a8ad-8fe2e16bae39`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $n$ be a natural number. The assertion is that for every formal Laurent series $f$ over
$\mathbb{Q}$ (an element of `LaurentSeries ℚ`, i.e. a Hahn series over $\mathbb{Q}$ with value group
$\mathbb{Z}$) satisfying `PoleOrderLE f n`, that is, $f$ has vanishing coefficient in degree $k$ for
every integer $k < -n$, there exists a polynomial $P \in \mathbb{Q}[X]$ with $\deg P \le n$ (in the
form `P.natDegree ≤ n`) such that `PoleOrderLE (f - Polynomial.aeval jq P) 0` holds, i.e. the
Laurent series obtained from $f$ by subtracting the value of $P$ at `jq` has vanishing coefficient
in every negative degree. Here `jq` is the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot \iota(\,$`jNumQ`$\,)$, the
product of the Hahn series `HahnSeries.single (-1) 1` with the image of the power series `jNumQ`
(the coefficientwise image in $\mathbb{Q}$ of the integral power series `jNum`) under the inclusion
of power series into Laurent series, and `Polynomial.aeval jq P` is the evaluation of $P$ at this
element of the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra of Laurent series. The quantifier over $f$ sits inside the
conclusion, so the statement is a single assertion about the number $n$.

This is the elementary approximation step underlying the description of the $q$-expansions of
level-one modular forms: a $q$-series with a pole of order at most $n$ at the cusp can be corrected
by a polynomial of degree at most $n$ in the $q$-expansion of $j$ so as to become holomorphic there.
It is used in the proof that a suitably invariant $q$-expansion lies in the algebra generated by
`jq`, via
[`ModularCurve.mem_adjoin_jq_of_hasSum_of_slash_invariant`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.mem_adjoin_jq_of_hasSum_of_slash_invariant).

